## What Happened

**Apple removed DSA support from its native security frameworks (Security.framework / CommonCrypto).** .NET on macOS relies on these native libraries for cryptographic primitives. Starting in .NET 7, `DSA` was marked obsolete (`SYSLIB0042`), and in .NET 11 the platform-specific implementation on macOS was fully removed — so `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS.

Linux still works because .NET uses OpenSSL there (which retains DSA support). Windows works because it uses CNG.

## How to Fix

Since you only need **verification** of legacy signatures, the cleanest cross-platform fix is to use a managed crypto library that doesn't depend on native OS frameworks:

**Option 1 — BouncyCastle (recommended):**

```bash
dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
```

```csharp
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var keyParam = (DsaPublicKeyParameters)PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA"); // or SHA256withDSA
    verifier.Init(false, keyParam);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

**Option 2 — Conditional OpenSSL on macOS:**

If you'd rather keep using the built-in `DSA` class on Linux/Windows, you can conditionally use BouncyCastle only on macOS:

```csharp
if (!OperatingSystem.IsMacOS())
{
    // Use built-in DSA (suppress SYSLIB0042 warning)
    #pragma warning disable SYSLIB0042
    using var dsa = DSA.Create();
    // ...
    #pragma warning restore SYSLIB0042
}
else
{
    // Use BouncyCastle as above
}
```

**Option 1 is preferred** — it gives you a single code path across all platforms and avoids the obsolete API entirely.
